<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">French Mandate for Syria and the Lebanon</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1946 CE · capital Beirut</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The League of Nations mandate under which France governed Syria and Lebanon between the world wars, shaping both modern states.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-remo-conference">San Remo conference</a> of April 1920 assigned <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lebanese-republic">Lebanon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. French forces overthrew the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arab-kingdom-of-syria">Arab Kingdom of Syria</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maysalun">Maysalun</a> that July, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> formally confirmed the mandate in 1923.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ruled through a high commissioner in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beirut">Beirut</a> and divided the territory into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/greater-lebanon">Greater Lebanon</a>, the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alawite-state">Alawite State</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jabal-druze-state">Jabal Druze State</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sanjak-of-alexandretta">Sanjak of Alexandretta</a>, often favouring minorities. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-syrian-revolt">Great Syrian Revolt</a> of 1925-1927, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-al-atrash">Sultan al-Atrash</a>, was crushed after French artillery shelled Damascus. A 1936 treaty promising independence was never ratified by France, and in 1939 Alexandretta was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After British and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-france">Free French</a> forces took the territories from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vichy-france">Vichy France</a> in 1941, independence was proclaimed. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lebanese-republic">Lebanon</a> gained real control in 1943, and the last French troops left <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> in April and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> in December 1946.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-mandate-for-syria-and-the-lebanon.jpg" alt="French Mandate for Syria and the Lebanon" width="320" height="528"><figcaption>Image: Mandate for Syria and the Lebanon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mandate_for_Syria_and_the_Lebanon.djvu" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Beirut</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henri Gouraud, Maurice Sarrail, Georges Catroux</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Christianity, Alawite and Druze communities</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French Mandate of Syria, Mandate for Syria and the Lebanon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1933&amp;focus=french-mandate-for-syria-and-the-lebanon" aria-label="French Mandate for Syria and the Lebanon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5585 949 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Mandate for Syria and the Lebanon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5905 1205L5927 1218M5905 1205L5054 728M5905 1205L5054 728M5905 1205L5927 1218M5905 1205L5936 1127M5905 1205L